        "level": 2,
        "text": "SQL Server Recovery Model"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "A recovery model is a database property that controls how transactions are logged and what restore operations are available."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Recovery Models"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Recovery Model", "Description", "Data Loss", "Point-in-time Recovery"],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Simple",
            "No log backups",
            "Changes since most recent backup lost",
            "Only to end of backup"
          ],
          ["Full", "Requires log backups", "Typically none", "Yes, to any point in time"],
          [
            "Bulk-Logged",
            "Requires log backups",
            "Minimal for bulk operations",
            "To end of any backup only"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Changing Recovery Model"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Change to SIMPLE recovery",
        "content": "Sets the recovery model to SIMPLE.",
        "language": "sql",
        "code": "ALTER DATABASE HR\nSET RECOVERY SIMPLE;"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Takeaways"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Recovery Model Key Points",
        "points": [
          "Simple recovery: no log backups, smallest log files.",
          "Full recovery: enables point-in-time recovery.",
          "Bulk-logged: optimized for bulk operations with minimal logging.",
          "Use ALTER DATABASE ... SET RECOVERY to change the model."
        ]
      }
    }
